# meta-car-ecu

This Yocto layer provides a recipe for building and packaging a C-based automotive ECU simulation application using the Yocto Project.

## Layer Contents

The layer contains the Yocto recipe and application source files required to build the `car-ecu` application.

### Directory Structure

```text
meta-car-ecu/
├── recipes-example/
│   └── car-ecu/
│       ├── car-ecu_1.0.bb
│       └── files/
│           └── <application source files>
└── README
```

## Dependencies

This layer depends on:

* OpenEmbedded-Core (`oe-core`)

Make sure the layer is used with a compatible Yocto/OpenEmbedded build environment.

## Application Source

The source files for the automotive ECU simulation application are included in:

```text
recipes-example/car-ecu/files/
```

The recipe uses these source files to build the application with the Yocto cross-compilation toolchain.

## Recipe

The main recipe is:

```text
recipes-example/car-ecu/car-ecu_1.0.bb
```

The recipe:

1. Fetches the application source files from the layer.
2. Compiles the C source code using the Yocto cross-compiler.
3. Installs the resulting executable into the target root filesystem.

The resulting application is installed as:

```text
/usr/bin/car_ecu_app
```

## Building the Application

After adding `meta-car-ecu` to your Yocto build environment, build the recipe using:

```bash
bitbake car-ecu
```

A successful build produces the `car_ecu_app` executable for the configured target architecture.

## Adding the Application to an Image

To include the application in a Yocto image, add the following to `local.conf`:

```bitbake
IMAGE_INSTALL:append = " car-ecu"
```

Then build the desired image, for example:

```bash
bitbake core-image-minimal
```

After booting the generated image, the application can be executed with:

```bash
car_ecu_app
```
